Requires the exact name in permissions.secrets. `secret.get` is hosted only (remote plugins receive owner secrets via `/ether/configure`); missing or expired secrets produce unavailable, and undeclared or revoked grants produce permission denied. Both hosted and remote plugins may store plugin-derived credentials (such as session cookies or short-lived tokens) with `secret.set(name, value, ttlSec?)` and remove them with `secret.delete(name)` under declared names that are not owner-configured settings fields, then reference them as `{secret:<name>}` in playable source headers and `http.hash`.

```ts
const token = ether.secret.get("apiKey");
ether.secret.set("sessionCookie", "sid=abc", 3600);
```

The declarations and implementations below specify arguments, return types, defaults, permission checks, limits, and errors. SDK adapters translate raw hosted calls to portable clients. See [SDKs](/plugins/reference/sdk-typescript) and [Errors](/plugins/reference/troubleshooting).

Hosted fetch requires the destination in permissions.network or in the owner's per-plugin local network grants. Redirect destinations are checked too, and credentials or caller headers follow redirects only within the same origin, to a strict same-site subdomain (verified via the Public Suffix List, no port change or HTTPS→HTTP downgrade), or to hosts explicitly listed in `keepHeadersOnRedirect` (still within granted network hosts, never across HTTPS→HTTP downgrades, and sticky once an untrusted hop is reached). The same redirect policy applies to `http.fetchMany`, `http.fetchToCache`, `http.hash`, and playable sources (`range`, `download`, `direct`, `container`). Use `ether.http.fetchMany(requests, {concurrency?})` to overlap up to 64 independent HTTP requests on the host (bounded to 8 in-flight per batch, 6 per origin across Ether, 10 MiB aggregate response bytes, and 100 units per request); results return in input order with either `{status, headers, body, bodyBase64?}` or `{error: {code, message}}`, and parent request cancellation aborts in-flight calls. `http.hash` accepts optional `headers` with `{secret:<name>}` expansion (no hop-by-hop/framing headers, never logged or cached). Read the generated MaxFetchBytes and transport limits; private LAN destinations fail unless the owner allows the exact host or host:port under On your network in the plugin's settings (exact local names in permissions.network are pre-granted at install, while loopback requires an explicit localhost or 127.0.0.1 grant). Handle upstream non-2xx status explicitly.

```ts
const r = ether.http.fetch({url: "https://api.example.org/status", method: "GET"});
if (r.status !== 200) throw new Error("Service unavailable");

const batch = ether.http.fetchMany(
  [
    {url: "https://a.example.org/item/1", timeoutMs: 1500},
    {url: "https://b.example.org/item/1", timeoutMs: 1500},
  ],
  {concurrency: 6},
);
const ok = batch.filter((item) => !item.error && item.status === 200);
```

